<section class="short-section">
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    In university group work, unequal contribution stays invisible until the deadline, when it is too late to do
    anything about it. The team framed group assessment as a black box of accountability and aimed the product at
    making it visible while there is still time to act.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="short-section">
  <h2>What we built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Structured task logging, so each member records what they did and for how long.</li>
    <li>
      A real-time equity indicator. After every logged contribution, a database trigger recalculates the team's balance
      from the gap between the highest and lowest contributor: under 30% shows green, 30 to 50% amber, above 50% red.
    </li>
    <li>Automatic imbalance alerts that move from active to acknowledged to resolved.</li>
    <li>A confidential flag for raising a concern about the team.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">The indicator relies on self-reported hours, a limit the report states plainly.</p>
</section>

<section class="short-section">
  <h2>My part</h2>
  <p>
    I was Technical Lead in a team of four. I wrote the problem statement and the product vision, built the MVP in
    Lovable and Supabase, and wrote the core logic with Claude Code: the equity trigger, alert generation and seed data.
    The project received 81.25%.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="short-section">
  <h2>What the team learned</h2>
  <figure class="report-quote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The key learning was that scope discipline mattered more than feature count: dropping SDG 8, reclassifying US-09
        to Must-Have mid-build, and deferring non-essential features produced a smaller but more reliable MVP than the
        original Sprint 1 backlog envisioned.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <figcaption>From the team's project report. US-09 is the automatic imbalance alert.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

